 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Credit card rates are far above the cash rate, and they have barely moved as the cash rate has changed. I couldn't get current personal loan data.\n\n**Credit cards (RBA indicator rates, advertised)**\n\n| Period | Cash rate (monthly avg) | Standard card | Low-rate card |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan 2025 | 4.35% | 20.99% | 13.49% |\n| Oct 2025 to Jan 2026 | 3.60% (the low) | 20.99% | 13.49% |\n| Sep 2026 (latest) | 4.36% | 21.24% | 13.49% |\n\n- **Gap:** In September 2026 the standard card rate was about 16.9 percentage points above the cash rate, roughly 4.9 times as high. The low-rate card was about 9.1 points above.\n- **Gap at the low point:** When the cash rate hit 3.60%, the standard card gap was about 17.4 points.\n- **Little pass-through:** The cash rate fell 0.75 points from January to October 2025, then rose 0.76 points from January to September 2026. The standard card rate stayed at 20.99% through all of that. It rose to 21.24% only in September 2026. The low-rate card has stayed at 13.49% throughout.\n- **Why:** Card rates are set mainly by credit risk and by how little most borrowers shop around. They aren't set mainly by funding costs.\n\n**Personal loans**\n- The RBA's fixed-rate unsecured personal loan series (F5) has no data after February 2020, when it was 12.46%. At the time, the cash rate was about 0.75%, so the gap was about 11.7 points.\n- The RBA has discontinued the series. I don't have a current personal loan figure from this source.\n- The RBA's F6 table of actual rates paid might cover personal loans. I didn't check it.\n\nThese are advertised rates. The rates borrowers actually pay can be lower.",
